____________________________________________________________________________________________________
FINAL STEADY STATE (no absorbing barrier)
____________________________________________________________________________________________________

J N = R F without a barrier: under continuous formation, after the stabilized population has reached its own
steady state, the yields of every exit, chemical activation and thermal reaction together (GO10 eqs. 7-9).

Not available: T = 300 K, p = 76 Torr: Banded Cholesky: non-positive pivot -3.2397903063093114e8 in row 460; the matrix is not positive definite. The symmetrized operator must be positive definite; in the final steady state this fails when the thermal rate coefficient is negligible compared with the collision frequency (near-singular J); use the intermediate steady state then.
Not available: T = 300 K, p = 228 Torr: Banded Cholesky: non-positive pivot -1.0084993194036441e9 in row 448; the matrix is not positive definite. The symmetrized operator must be positive definite; in the final steady state this fails when the thermal rate coefficient is negligible compared with the collision frequency (near-singular J); use the intermediate steady state then.
Not available: T = 300 K, p = 760 Torr: relative residual 1.6046906901747096e0 of J N = F exceeds the tolerance 1e-8. In the final steady state this means that J is numerically singular: the thermal rate coefficient of a well without a bimolecular sink is negligible compared with the collision frequency (the final steady state is reached only after about 0.1/k_uni, O02 text after eq. 13); use the intermediate steady state for such conditions.
