____________________________________________________________________________________________________
THERMAL RATE COEFFICIENTS OF THE FINAL STEADY STATE (lowest eigenpair of J)
____________________________________________________________________________________________________

k_uni = sum of the specific rate coefficients averaged over the normalized eigenvector of the lowest eigenvalue
lambda_1 of J (Gonzalez-Garcia, Olzmann, PCCP 12, 12290 (2010), text after eq. 12); lambda_1 = k_uni in exact
arithmetic (eq. 12), checked as the sum rule (warning above 1.5e-2). Eigen-solver: FullDecompositionLapack. The thermal branching is
k_th(channel)/k_uni: the yields of the thermal decay of the network.

Warning, T = 300 K, p = 76 torr: sum rule lambda_1 = k_uni violated: lambda_1 = 2.1296526345116138e-7 s-1, k_uni = 5.904778473955666e-16 s-1, relative deviation 3.607e8 > 1.5e-2. k_uni is the average of the specific rate coefficients over the normalized thermal eigenvector (Gonzalez-Garcia, Olzmann, Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys. 12, 12290 (2010), text after eq. 12), equal to the lowest eigenvalue lambda_1 (eq. 12) in exact arithmetic. Here lambda_1 is not resolved in double precision (it lies far below the collision frequency and the largest k(E)); the eigenvector, and with it k_uni, is much less sensitive. What to do: use k_uni, and confirm it with the inverse iteration (the most accurate solver for the thermal eigenvector); if the solvers give different k_uni, the condition is beyond double precision.

Warning, T = 300 K, p = 228 torr: sum rule lambda_1 = k_uni violated: lambda_1 = -6.565269240841449e-7 s-1, k_uni = 7.425205631549776e-16 s-1, relative deviation 8.842e8 > 1.5e-2. The lowest eigenvalue is not positive, which is impossible for J when population can leave the network (all eigenvalues are positive, GO10 text before eq. 12): lambda_1 = -6.565269240841449e-7 s-1 is rounding noise below the double-precision floor eps*max(S_ii) = 1.0043914988253659e-2 s-1. This is not a merging of eigenvalues (thermal decay indistinguishable from relaxation): the separation lambda_2/k_uni = 3.333992420027275e23 is large; merging would show as a small lambda_2/k_uni. k_uni is the average of the specific rate coefficients over the normalized thermal eigenvector (Gonzalez-Garcia, Olzmann, Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys. 12, 12290 (2010), text after eq. 12), equal to the lowest eigenvalue lambda_1 (eq. 12) in exact arithmetic. Here lambda_1 is not resolved in double precision (it lies far below the collision frequency and the largest k(E)); the eigenvector, and with it k_uni, is much less sensitive. What to do: use k_uni, and confirm it with the inverse iteration (the most accurate solver for the thermal eigenvector); if the solvers give different k_uni, the condition is beyond double precision.

